WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.100
DENISE WINKLER: Hello.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.480
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:09.850
My name is Denise Winkler.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.030
I'm a strategic business
executive with Google Cloud.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.270
And I'm pleased to introduce
our guest and client Jennifer

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.630
Ricker, assistant secretary
for the Illinois Department

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.460
of Innovation and Technology.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.190
In today's session, we're going
to outline the programs that

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:34.210
make up the social safety net,
the impact that COVID-19 has

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.590
had on citizens and the social
safety net system, the Google

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.450
solutions that
have been mobilized

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.870
to assist these agencies.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.530
And Jennifer's going to share
with us how Google Contact

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.370
Center AI supported the Illinois
Department of Employment

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.990
Security.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.820
Then we'll close
with a discussion

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.480
on how Google Analytics can
help our communities to reopen

00:00:59.480 --> 00:00:59.980
and recover.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.560
More than 54 million citizens
applied for unemployment

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.910
in the last 20 weeks.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:15.020
As we implemented stay at home
orders and social distancing,

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.140
citizens were directed to
portals and call centers

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.480
to apply for safety
net services.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.290
This unprecedented volume of
citizens in need of services

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:31.230
has overwhelmed agency websites,
call centers, and staff.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:35.510
Social safety net programs
provide critical services,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:40.460
unemployment, food, cash,
and housing assistance,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:44.810
health care, child support,
and protective services

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.700
for abused children
and the elderly.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.840
Leading economists are
projecting that more than 40%

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.430
of the jobs will not come back.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.840
So caseload sizes in these
social safety net programs

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.105
are going to remain
at record highs.

00:02:01.105 --> 00:02:04.720
And in addition to that,
state and local governments

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.810
are facing record
revenue shortfalls.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.870
Reduced tax revenue,
high unemployment,

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.770
and just the cost of
responding to the pandemic

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.820
may force many states to
make painful spending cuts.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.060
So, essentially, local
and state governments

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.910
will need to serve this
unprecedented number

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.550
of citizens with less resources.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.040
We've all been
impacted by COVID-19.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.440
But as we look across the social
safety net and the stakeholders

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.550
involved in this process,
citizens have lost their jobs

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.690
or become ill.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.970
They need these
benefits quickly.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.820
And the portals and call
centers are overwhelmed.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.330
And many of these
individuals and families

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.500
have never applied
for services before.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.910
So they need basic
guidance on how to apply

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.120
or even what benefits
they're eligible for.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.390
And they're also facing the
uncertainty of relief programs.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.680
What will be available?

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.050
What won't be available?

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.460
Employees of these agencies are
also overwhelmed by the volume.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.830
They've had to
almost immediately

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:18.820
change and implement new
programs and new business

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.260
processes.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:23.860
Many of them are working from
home for the first time ever.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.620
They may lack the appropriate
equipment or training

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.270
to do that well.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:34.160
And like us, many of them have
children and families at home.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.620
The agencies themselves,
they're struggling

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.060
with their current
legacy systems.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.280
These systems were never built
to manage this kind of volume.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.830
And they also can't
quickly be enhanced

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.340
to support new programs
like Pandemic Unemployment

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.690
Assistance.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.390
So as a result, many
of these agencies

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.100
and their administrators
are facing

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.570
significant public and
political pressure.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.570
And in addition to that,
they've had to, again,

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.050
almost overnight
develop new processes,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.360
enable their staff
to work remotely.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.500
And that's a huge culture change
from what is predominantly

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.790
been an in-office culture.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.440
And then, finally,
these agencies

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.529
have a responsibility
to make sure

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:22.170
that the benefits are being
distributed to citizens

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:29.090
who are eligible and to prevent
improper payments and fraud.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.610
As we've worked with
local and state agencies

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.490
across the country, we
notice that they're actually

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.540
facing four primary problems,
volume, new programs

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.900
and legislation, how do
they engage virtually,

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.540
then reopening and recovery.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.390
And Google has mobilized
a series of solutions

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.920
to address these issues.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.240
Volume, for example, we've
implemented virtual waiting

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:00.810
rooms that control traffic
to public-facing portals.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.200
But more importantly, they
provide better service,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.350
and they help calm citizens.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.000
They calm them,
one, by providing

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.700
a clock using AI
and machine learning

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:15.750
to tell them how long they
have to wait to apply.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:19.500
They also provide information
by bringing forward FAQs

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.690
so they can spend
their time effectively

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.320
while they're waiting.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.490
We've implemented very
sophisticated virtual agents

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.680
on both portals
and call centers.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.600
And these are not your first
generation automated FAQs

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.500
or tinny mechanical voices.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.040
Using artificial intelligence
and a highly developed

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.760
natural language
conversational engines,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.450
these virtual agents
can answer questions

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:54.110
and complete routine tasks with
really human-like engagement.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.500
And Jennifer's going to
talk about her experiences

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:01.210
with our intelligent
agents coming up next.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.850
We've also used AI
and machine learning.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:09.490
We've automated a great deal
of processing of documents.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.310
Government programs,
especially safety net programs,

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:15.130
begin and end with documents.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.790
And many of these agencies given
the increase in applications

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:25.040
are drowning in paper documents
and all sorts of documents.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.230
So Google can create
and train a model

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:32.440
that can digitize and
ingest a document,

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.790
extract that data
off the document,

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.570
whether the document is
handwritten or typed.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.060
It can pull the data off
and put it into fields.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.950
It can index the
documents to a case, then

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.030
validate and match
that document.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:51.850
So for example, if a citizen
has submitted an application

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:55.930
and a verification document like
a driver's license or a utility

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:59.330
bill, it can match
and verify that.

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.840
And then it can
automatically input that data

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.020
into your core case
management system.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:11.200
This enables agencies to
redeploy those human agents

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.490
to higher value activities and
help citizens work through much

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.040
more difficult problems.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.210
With new legislation, we've
been able to rapidly develop

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.510
applications and
alternative data collection

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.660
for new programs like
the Pandemic Unemployment

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.960
Assistance or improper payments.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.070
And then more importantly, we
can integrate that module back

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.240
into the legacy system.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.480
So this enables agencies
to quickly administer

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.340
these new programs, control
volume to their website

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.220
and data to their portals.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:56.540
With virtual engagement,
our virtual collaboration

00:07:56.540 --> 00:08:00.920
and conferencing solution
G Suite's Google Meet

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:05.540
enables agencies to not only
support a remote workforce

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.570
but enable virtual service
delivery to citizens.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:11.630
Whether it's meetings
and collaboration

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:15.860
between coworkers, between
government staff and community

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.750
providers, or providing
virtual services to citizens,

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.150
Google Meet is
ideal for government

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.670
to citizen interaction
because it only

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:29.200
requires a web-enabled device.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.890
And when we come to
reopen and recovery,

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:37.419
states are going to need to
look for efficiencies to manage

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.970
the continued high volume.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.650
The solutions we've
already talked

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:46.600
about, such as intelligent
agents, automatic document

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:49.870
processing, rapid
AI, those can be

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:54.970
expanded across the government
ecosystem to other programs

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.380
to help gain those efficiencies.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:03.000
In addition, other solutions
are going to be needed.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.380
Getting people back to
work will be critical.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:08.220
So virtual career
centers that can help

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:10.980
support a much higher
volume of citizens

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.730
who are seeking reentry
into the job market.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.150
Given the number of citizens
and the dollars associated

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.580
with these programs,
agencies will

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.110
need solutions that identify
improper payments and fraud.

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:28.530
And then finally, and
probably most importantly,

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:32.850
data analytics will be critical.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.550
And we're going to cover
some data analytics options

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.130
at the end of our session.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:44.480
But it's always nice to hear
about outcomes from clients.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:47.650
So Jennifer would you
share some of the issues

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:51.400
that the Illinois Department of
Employment Security was facing

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:55.923
and how Google Contact Center
AI was able to help your agency.

00:09:55.923 --> 00:09:56.840
JENNIFER RICKER: Sure.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:57.860
Thank you, Denise.

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:01.070
And thank you again for letting
me have the opportunity here

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:06.020
to share the challenges
that Illinois specifically

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.350
was facing and, in particular,
the Illinois Department

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:11.060
of Employment Security.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:15.350
So like what you just
described, the situation

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:18.190
that was happening
across the country

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:22.010
was the same really
for Illinois.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.730
Shortly after the
pandemic began,

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:31.010
we began to see a large,
really unprecedented volume

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:36.080
of inquiries and
activities on our website,

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.100
calls to our contact
center, and attempts

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:44.300
to file new
unemployment requests.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:47.900
And these changes were really--

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.810
the amount, the volumes
that we were seeing

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:53.100
have never been experienced
before in the state.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.570
So to say we weren't
necessarily prepared for that

00:10:56.570 --> 00:10:57.690
was an understatement.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:03.020
So we're talking about really
orders of magnitude difference.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:06.800
An average day prior to the
pandemic for our website,

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:10.030
for IDES's website would
have been maybe 40,000 hits

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:12.620
in a day versus what
we started to see,

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:19.475
a million hits in a day, which
quickly impacted our servers

00:11:19.475 --> 00:11:23.120
and the system itself
that was trying

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:24.380
to process the applications.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.800
So on the other
end of that, then,

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.530
when our residents were
unable to get to the website,

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.690
get their questions
answered there,

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:36.050
then they start calling
the contact center, which

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:38.600
then overwhelms the
agents on the phones

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.930
and overwhelms that
system as well.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.510
So it really became
just a snowball effect

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:47.630
for all channels.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:51.050
And I'll mention, too, that
one of the reasons this also

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:55.010
snowballed-- previously,
the Department of Employment

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.230
Security like a lot
of our state agencies

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:01.610
was still dealing with a lot
of very manual, very paper

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:05.660
processes, a lot of walk-ins
at their local offices,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:09.980
which, obviously, with the
shut down, those had to close.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.250
That contact and that avenue
of connecting with the state

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:15.420
was closed to our residents.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.990
So, again, that helped
to drive the volumes

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:21.770
that we were seeing on the
website and on the phones.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:25.490
So really as we
started to look at what

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:29.600
are the solutions for this,
thinking about the volumes

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.760
that we were receiving,
there was absolutely

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.960
no way we were going to, one,
hire enough agents quick enough

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:40.250
and train them quick enough
to handle this kind of volume.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:43.790
So frankly, this type of
technology becoming that force

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.790
multiplier was the only
solution, the quickest

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:48.750
solution, and the best solution.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:51.890
So the way we handled
that was really

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:56.420
to begin to work with
Google and really implement

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:01.490
this virtual agent
contact center technology.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:04.592
And it was, I will
say, much easier

00:13:04.592 --> 00:13:06.050
than I would have
expected, quicker

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.880
than I would have expected.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.060
And we were very
successful with that.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.640
So the first phase
was really deploying

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:16.400
that and what we thought
could be the quickest

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.100
and what would have the
biggest impact first.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.070
And that was really
on our website

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:24.710
and deploying the
chatbot onto the website.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:27.200
Again, that was
highly successful.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.440
I will say within the first
two weeks of operation,

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:35.660
I think it handled three
million or more web inquiries.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:37.640
We were also able to
verify as you talked

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.490
about Google's Analytics
and the ability

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:44.690
to track really what's
happening behind the scenes.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:47.120
We were able to pull
the data from there

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.330
and take a look at
dashboards to know

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:54.470
that not only did it handle
that number of inquiries,

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:59.060
but those were almost
without fail successful.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.360
So the questions
that were being asked

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.870
were being answered
successfully.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:10.340
And we also were able to
very easily add new intents

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.910
and change those
intents, as you mentioned

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:15.980
the legislation
that changed quite

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.750
frequently on the federal side.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:25.190
So as those changes
happened, it was much easier

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:30.180
to add those changes, intents
and add new intents to the bot

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:33.680
so that residents as they
have new questions about,

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.870
OK, this just changed, or
how do I apply for this,

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:39.230
or do I qualify for
this, it's really easy

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.420
rather than trying to
search through the website,

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:45.050
they can just easily ask the
question in, as you mentioned,

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:49.910
a very conversational
manner with the web bot.

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:50.960
And then same thing.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.430
After we got that up and
running and successful,

00:14:55.430 --> 00:15:01.470
we moved on to our contact
center, again, very easy,

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.990
although we did integrate
with our technology

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.880
that we utilized to
run our contact center.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.610
Again, it was relatively easy.

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:10.950
We've been able
to utilize, again,

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:12.310
the same type of intense.

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:13.980
It's very conversational.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:17.170
People are able to
speak to the agent.

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:22.290
So that has been really
surprisingly very, very much

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:24.320
beneficial to us as well.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:29.610
And I would also mention
that previously IDES,

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:34.200
their contact center was
a during the day type

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.110
of operation.

00:15:35.110 --> 00:15:38.400
So adding this virtual
agent to the phone

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.140
allowed those
questions to continue

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:42.720
to be answered after
hours, all night long,

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:46.120
over the weekends,
et cetera, so, again,

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:49.260
the ability for us to really
quickly implement these.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:50.550
They're easy to scale.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:55.800
They're easy to continue
to manage and maintain.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.150
Has been phenomenal for us.

00:15:57.150 --> 00:16:00.780
And we continue to expand
this to other agencies

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.270
as well, other
agencies' websites.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:07.530
So we're really pleased
with how this has really

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:09.270
rolled out and been deployed.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.370
So thank you again
for letting me share.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.170
And I'll turn it
back to you, Denise.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.850
DENISE WINKLER:
Thank you, Jennifer.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:21.140
In the midst of one of the
largest surges of citizens

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.840
who need services,
your story really

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:27.440
demonstrates the unique
capability and value

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:29.720
of Google Cloud technology.

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:32.250
It can scale to
petabytes of data,

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.420
enabling states to manage
that extraordinary volume.

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:39.620
The time to deliver, we were
able to deliver these solutions

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:42.530
in days and weeks, not months.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:45.930
It's also serverless, so there's
no hardware installation.

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:48.330
And I think integration
is also important.

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:51.500
We were able to modernize
critical components

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:54.290
and then integrate them
with an existing legacy

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:58.340
system, which provides
the cost effectiveness.

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:01.820
Our approach to public sector
is to be cost effective

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.220
but also to make that
cost predictable.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:07.400
And so these are the principles
that support governments

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.950
in modernizing around some
of these legacy systems

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.760
in dealing with
this current issue.

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:16.430
So let's spend a
little time talking

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:20.160
about how we can support
recovery with analytics.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:24.109
Everyone, citizens and leaders,
want to reopen and recover.

00:17:24.109 --> 00:17:28.640
But one of the key components
of success is data.

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:31.550
As a 20-year veteran
of social services,

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:36.020
I realized that the
social safety net

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.900
agencies could be overwhelmed.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:42.170
But providing the data to
state and local leaders

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:44.840
to address that challenge
would be difficult.

00:17:44.840 --> 00:17:48.350
So I engaged with
Google Cloud Value

00:17:48.350 --> 00:17:51.260
Advisors, our center
of excellence,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:56.180
to work with federal and state
publicly available data sets so

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:59.780
that we could provide
dashboards that would help

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:06.170
estimate the potential economic
impact of relief programs

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:10.460
and the potential increase
for citizen applications

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:14.690
and involvement in
safety net programs.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.100
And this type of data
would help state leaders

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.240
be able to develop data-driven
plans to support citizens

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:23.190
in our communities.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:26.160
So, initially, we thought
that leaders needed insights

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.170
into four key areas.

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:31.260
It would help to understand
the vulnerable industry sectors

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.150
in their state or community.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.240
Since economists have
estimated that 40% of the jobs

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:40.560
won't recover, how much of
your state's employment basis

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.320
is at risk?

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:45.100
Second, given the high
level of unemployment,

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:47.740
what percentage of
your state's economy

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:50.930
is coming from temporary
relief programs

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.520
and from traditional
safety net programs?

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:58.480
Thirdly, what is the impact
to your state's economy

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:02.420
when relief programs
are added or expired?

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:07.850
And then, also, what is the gap
when temporary relief programs

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:09.510
are gone?

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:13.110
So this particular dashboard,
Employment Vulnerability--

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.140
our Cloud Value
Advisors developed

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:20.430
a series of dashboards to
visualize these insights.

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:23.760
This particular dashboard
shows the percentage

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:28.560
of vulnerable industry sectors
nationally and also by state.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:31.470
So for example,
leisure and hospitality

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:34.410
are vulnerable as
well as retail.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:41.100
This dashboard estimates that
39.1% of the US employment

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.200
is in vulnerable industries.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:46.365
This type of data can
also be shown by states.

00:19:46.365 --> 00:19:48.930
So it would enable
state and local leaders

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:53.690
to develop much more
effective recovery plans

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:57.690
for their particular community.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:00.120
The Contribution of Benefits.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:03.540
There are a multitude
of state, federal,

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:06.230
and local relief programs
that are providing benefits

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:07.370
to citizens.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.950
But at the same
time, those benefits

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:13.400
are contributing to that
community's economy.

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:16.640
So using the Federal Pandemic
Unemployment Compensation

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:22.550
as an example, this
dashboard estimates how much

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:25.130
Federal Pandemic
Unemployment Compensation

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:28.760
contributes to a state's
gross domestic product.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:31.610
So it provides
leaders with insights

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:34.980
into the economic impact
of those programs.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:38.930
So, again, leaders can develop
the policies to address

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:41.270
those specific issues.

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:44.180
And then Economic
Impact and Surge.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:47.570
As different relief
programs expire,

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.990
citizens may be eligible
for other programs.

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:54.890
This dashboard estimates the
potential surge, for example,

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:58.820
in food assistance applications
as the Pandemic Unemployment

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:01.490
Compensation expires.

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:04.520
This will enable leaders
to be better prepared

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:07.080
for the impact of these surges.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:11.480
At Google, we spent a great
deal of time discussing

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:15.440
how technology can
assist social safety net

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:19.160
agencies, and our
communities, and country

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.920
in reopening and recovery.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.710
But in closing, I think
it's important to keep

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:28.190
in mind that when systems
that support social safety net

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:33.500
programs slow or fail, there's
a very real human cost.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.090
Children go hungry.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:36.830
Rent can't be paid.

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:38.810
Lives are impacted.

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:41.360
Keeping these
systems functioning

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:45.740
is critical to our citizens, our
communities, and our recovery.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:49.550
And Google is committed to
assisting wherever it can.

00:21:49.550 --> 00:21:52.270
Thank you for your time today.

